WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
There are several situations where you should always call 911 immediately.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
This includes if someone is unresponsive, experiencing chest discomfort,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.000
or showing signs of a stroke.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
You should also call for severe injuries like deep burns or heavy bleeding,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
seizures, electric shocks, or if someone has been exposed to poison.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Anytime there is a problem with breathing, a suspected head, neck, or back injury,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
a possible broken bone, or complications related to pregnancy,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
911 is your first priority.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
This also applies if you see blood in someone's stool or vomit.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
When you get a dispatcher on the line, be sure to give them your exact location

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.000
and answer all of their questions clearly.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Don't worry about the questions slowing things down.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Answering them actually helps help get to you faster.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Try to put your phone on speaker so you can keep your hands free

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
to provide care while following the dispatcher's instructions.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.000
It is a great idea to memorize the addresses of places you visit often,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
like your office or a gym, so you can give that information instantly during an emergency.

